Claude:
Great starting point. "Money that disappears" is actually a really specific and useful framing — it's the classic problem of small, frequent, forgettable transactions that don't feel significant individually but wreck a budget collectively.
Here are some directions to think about:
Catching "invisible" spending

Flag transactions under $10–15 that happen more than X times a week — the death-by-a-thousand-cuts category
Identify subscriptions and recurring charges (same merchant, similar amount, regular interval)
Spot "lazy spending" — food delivery, convenience stores, vending machines — things bought nearby when cheaper options exist
Highlight late-night transactions (a proxy for impulse spending)

Making the budget feel real

A "daily allowance" view — takes monthly budget and shows how much you have per day left this week
A "if you cut X, you could afford Y" insight — e.g. "cutting 3 coffees a week funds a weekend trip in 2 months"
A savings runway — at current spend rate, how many weeks until savings goal is hit?

Protecting social spending

Let the student define a "social budget" category — nights out, restaurants, events — and treat it as protected, not flagged as waste
Show social spend as a percentage so it feels justified, not guilty
Compare social spend to other "optional" categories so they can see the trade-offs themselves

Spending pattern insights

Day-of-week breakdown — do they spend more on Fridays? After payday?
Merchant frequency map — top 10 places money goes, ranked
"Bad week" detection — weeks where spend spiked vs their own average

Motivation / goal tracking

Holiday/goal savings tracker — "you're 34% of the way to Bali"
Streak tracking — "3 weeks under budget on food"
A simple "financial health score" that students can improve over time

